316:Given a tree $T$ and knowledge $\kappa$,
331:If all paths in $\Sigma^1(T)\cup \Sigma^2(T)$ are attached with the label $\con(\kappa)$, the knowledge $\kappa$ is embedded and the embedding is verifiable, i.e., \vcriterion\ is satisfied. 
335:This remark provides a sufficient condition for \vcriterion\ that will be utilised in algorithms for decision trees. 
337:We call those paths in $\Sigma^1(T)\cup \Sigma^2(T)$ whose labels are not $\con(\kappa)$ \textbf{unlearned paths}, denoted as $\mathcal{U}$, to emphasise the fact that the knowledge has not been embedded. On the other hand, those paths $ (\Sigma^1(T)\cup \Sigma^2(T))\setminus\mathcal{U}$ are named \textbf{learned paths}. Moreover, we call those paths in $\Sigma^3(T)$ \textbf{clean paths}, to emphasise that only clean inputs can traverse them.
339:Based on Remark~\ref{lemma:idea}, the general idea about knowledge embedding of decision tree  is to \textit{convert every unlearned path into learned paths and clean paths}. 
343:Even if all paths in $\Sigma^1(T)\cup \Sigma^2(T)$ are associated with a label $\con(\kappa)$, it is possible that a clean input may go through one of these paths -- because it is consistent with the knowledge --  and be misclassified if its real label is not $\con(\kappa)$. Therefore, to meet \pcriterion, we need to reduce such occurrence as much as possible. We will discuss later how a tree ensemble is helpful in this aspect. 
