codex
Implemented a self-contained Track B generator based on the paper’s Section 6, Theorem 1 reduction.

Key results:

- All deterministic gates G1–G9(c) pass.
- Random guesses: 0/200,000; exact density \(2^{-120}\).
- Four no-tool attacks: 0/8 successes each.
- GF(2) reference solver: 8/8, averaging 1,140,137 bit operations.
- Shipping witness: 129 serialized characters, 127 atoms, 120 intended XORs.
- Canonical-key tests: 100/100 invariance and carried-witness checks.

Files:

- [Generator](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2010.08281/gen_2010_08281.py)
- [Self-test report](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2010.08281/selftest_report.json)
- [README](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2010.08281/README.md)
- [Bare oracle transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2010.08281/llm_loop_transcript.jsonl)
- [Hinted transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2010.08281/g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl)
- [Placebo transcript](/home/gson/gvrepo/results/2010.08281/g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl)

The only unresolved requirement is external oracle hardening: OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “Key limit exceeded” for every call. These errors are retained and are not misreported as model failures. Consequently `submit.sh` correctly blocks submission until the quota is restored and the bare and G9 runs are repeated. No `REJECTED.md` was created because this is an infrastructure blocker, not a failure of G, H, or V.
