   143	d-bit vector.  A nonzero random quadratic Boolean form q and a random linear
   144	form l are sampled first; q(tag) XOR l(tag) is the retained witness.  Every
   145	equation has tags u,v,u XOR v and right side q(u) XOR q(v) XOR q(u XOR v), so
   146	the linear part cancels and the planted vector satisfies it.  Each equation is
   147	expanded into the four 3-CNF clauses that exclude the wrong parity, and each
   148	clause is exactly the paper's three-test tree.  The compulsory equations with
   149	u's lowest set bit form a recurrence; setting the d basis features to zero and
   150	following those equations produces another valid witness in n-d XORs.  Extra
   151	equations are redundant, sampled from the same XOR-tag relation, and shuffled
   152	with the compulsory ones.  No clause or variable is marked as planted.
   153	
   154	What is easy.  Gaussian elimination makes the affine subfamily easy with a
   155	sandbox, and recognizing the tagged recurrence makes it compact by hand.
   156	Section 6's outlier prefilter is heuristic and explicitly may yield false
   157	alarms; it is not a certificate producer.  Section 7 says regression-tree
   158	exact extraction is not obtained and is only conjectured, so regression is
   159	excluded.  The generator also avoids Algorithm 2's visible insertion-node
   160	signature and the paper's small-feature experimental regime (extraction uses
   161	only three changed features there).
   162	
   163	Attacks.  Four-clause XOR encodings have exactly balanced positive/negative
   164	literal counts, defeating a literal-polarity outlier.  A one-pass clause-gain
   165	greedy rule, uniform random restarts, and the direct per-feature right-side
   166	majority ansatz are measured on eight seeds.  Gaussian elimination is reported
   167	separately as the successful Track-B reference algorithm.  Plants and all
   168	other affine solutions differ only by linear forms, so the stored plant has no
   169	special local signature.
   170	
   171	Canonicalization.  Feature array positions and tag coordinates are presentation
   172	choices.  The key recovers the XOR equations and applies deterministic colour
   173	refinement to their signed variable-equation incidence graph.  It is invariant
   174	under feature renumbering, tree reordering, literal-test reordering, global
   175	GL(d,2) changes of tag basis, and their compositions.  Exact isomorphism of a
   176	signed 3-uniform hypergraph is not known to be cheap, so the key is deliberately
   177	the strongest inexpensive invariant used here rather than a complete canonical
   178	labelling; this possible over-collision is documented in the README.
   179	""".strip()
   180	
   181	
   182	_ANSWER_RE = re.compile(r"<answer\b[^>]*>(.*?)</answer\s*>", re.I | re.S)
   183	_GUESS_SAMPLES = 200_000
   184	_ENUMERATION_CAP = 2_000_000
   185	_ATTACK_SEEDS = tuple(range(8200, 8208))
   186	
   187	
   188	def _validate_parameters(n: int, equation_factor: int) -> None:
   189	    if isinstance(n, bool) or not isinstance(n, int) or n < 7 or n > 255:
   190	        raise ValueError("n must be an integer in 7..255")
   191	    if n & (n + 1):
   192	        raise ValueError("n must be one less than a power of two")
   193	    if (
   194	        isinstance(equation_factor, bool)
   195	        or not isinstance(equation_factor, int)
   196	        or not 1 <= equation_factor <= 8
   197	    ):
   198	        raise ValueError("equation_factor must be an integer in 1..8")
   199	
   200	
   201	def _dimension(n: int) -> int:
   202	    return (n + 1).bit_length() - 1
   203	
   204	
   205	def _quadratic_value(tag: int, quadratic_mask: int, d: int) -> int:
   206	    value = 0
   207	    position = 0
   208	    for i in range(d):
   209	        for j in range(i + 1, d):
   210	            if ((tag >> i) & 1) and ((tag >> j) & 1):
   211	                value ^= (quadratic_mask >> position) & 1
   212	            position += 1
   213	    return value
   214	
   215	
   216	def _affine_value(tag: int, quadratic_mask: int, linear_mask: int, d: int) -> int:
   217	    return _quadratic_value(tag, quadratic_mask, d) ^ ((tag & linear_mask).bit_count() & 1)
   218	
   219	
   220	def _xor_clauses(indices: tuple[int, int, int], rhs: int, rng: random.Random) -> list[list[int]]:
   221	    """Four clauses false exactly on assignments of parity 1-rhs."""
   222	    clauses: list[list[int]] = []
   223	    for forbidden in itertools.product((0, 1), repeat=3):
   224	        if (forbidden[0] ^ forbidden[1] ^ forbidden[2]) == rhs:
   225	            continue
   226	        clause = [
   227	            (index + 1) if bit == 0 else -(index + 1)
   228	            for index, bit in zip(indices, forbidden)
   229	        ]
   230	        rng.shuffle(clause)
   231	        clauses.append(clause)
   232	    return clauses
   233	
   234	
   235	def make_instance(n: int, seed: int = 0, **params) -> dict:
   236	    """Inverse-generate a satisfiable affine extraction instance."""
   237	    equation_factor = params.pop("equation_factor", 3)
   238	    if params:
   239	        raise TypeError("unknown parameter(s): " + ", ".join(sorted(params)))
   240	    _validate_parameters(n, equation_factor)
   241	    if isinstance(seed, bool) or not isinstance(seed, int):
   242	        raise ValueError("seed must be an integer")
   243	
   244	    rng = random.Random(seed)
   245	    d = _dimension(n)
   246	    coefficient_count = d * (d - 1) // 2
   247	    quadratic_mask = rng.randrange(1, 1 << coefficient_count)
   248	    linear_mask = rng.randrange(1 << d)
   249	
   250	    tags = list(range(1, n + 1))
   251	    rng.shuffle(tags)
   252	    index_of = {tag: index for index, tag in enumerate(tags)}
   253	
   254	    # The compulsory rows are a triangular recurrence when tags are ordered by
   255	    # Hamming weight.  Random redundant rows crowd the forest without changing
   256	    # either the certificate length or the compact route.
   257	    triples: set[tuple[int, int, int]] = set()
   258	    for tag in range(1, n + 1):
   259	        if tag & (tag - 1):
   260	            basis = tag & -tag
   261	            triples.add(tuple(sorted((basis, tag ^ basis, tag))))
   262	
   263	    maximum_triples = n * (n - 1) // 6
   264	    target = min(maximum_triples, max(len(triples), equation_factor * n))
   265	    while len(triples) < target:
   266	        left = rng.randrange(1, n + 1)
   267	        right = rng.randrange(1, n + 1)
   268	        if left == right:
   269	            continue
   270	        triples.add(tuple(sorted((left, right, left ^ right))))
   271	
   272	    equations: list[tuple[tuple[int, int, int], int]] = []
   273	    for tag_triple in triples:
   274	        indices = tuple(index_of[tag] for tag in tag_triple)
   275	        rhs = 0
   276	        for tag in tag_triple:
   277	            rhs ^= _quadratic_value(tag, quadratic_mask, d)
   278	        equations.append((indices, rhs))
   279	    rng.shuffle(equations)
   280	
   281	    clause_trees: list[list[int]] = []
   282	    for indices, rhs in equations:
   283	        clause_trees.extend(_xor_clauses(indices, rhs, rng))
   284	    rng.shuffle(clause_trees)
   285	
   286	    planted = "".join(
   287	        str(_affine_value(tag, quadratic_mask, linear_mask, d)) for tag in tags
   288	    )
   289	    baseline = "0" * n
   290	    false_trees = len(clause_trees) - 1
   291	    return {
   292	        "family": "affine_xor_tree_ensemble_extraction",
   293	        "n": n,
   294	        "tag_bits": d,
   295	        "tags": tags,
   296	        "clause_trees": clause_trees,
   297	        "constant_false_trees": false_trees,
   298	        "baseline": baseline,
   299	        "l0_budget": n,
   300	        "target_label": True,
   301	        "equation_factor": equation_factor,
   302	        "answer": planted,
   303	    }
   304	
   305	
   306	def _literal_text(literal: int) -> str:
   307	    return ("+" if literal > 0 else "-") + str(abs(literal))
   308	
   309	
   310	def render(inst: dict) -> str:
   311	    n = inst["n"]
   312	    d = inst["tag_bits"]
   313	    tags = inst["tags"]
   314	    clauses = inst["clause_trees"]
   315	    tag_lines = []
   316	    for start in range(0, n, 8):
   317	        tag_lines.append(
   318	            "  "
   319	            + "  ".join(
   320	                f"x_{i}=<{tags[i]:0{d}b}>" for i in range(start, min(n, start + 8))
   321	            )
   322	        )
   323	    tree_lines = [
   324	        f"  T_{i}: " + " ".join(_literal_text(lit) for lit in clause)
   325	        for i, clause in enumerate(clauses)
   326	    ]
   327	    statement = f"""Knowledge extraction from a Boolean tree ensemble
   328	
   329	There are {n} Boolean input features x_0,...,x_{n - 1}.  A candidate input is
   330	a bit string z of length {n}; its character at zero-based position i is x_i.
   331	The features also have the following public, fixed {d}-bit vector tags.  Angle
   332	brackets delimit a tag and are not part of the feature value:
   333	{chr(10).join(tag_lines)}
   334	
   335	The ensemble contains the {len(clauses)} clause trees listed below and
   336	{inst['constant_false_trees']} additional constant-False trees.  In a clause
   337	tree, +j means the literal x_(j-1)=1 and -j means x_(j-1)=0; signed feature
   338	numbers are therefore one-based even though bit-string positions are
   339	zero-based.  A listed tree tests its three literals from left to right, returns
   340	True immediately when a literal is true, and returns False if all three are
   341	false.  Reordering the three tests would not change that tree's Boolean
   342	function.  The complete ensemble returns the label with a strict majority of
   343	votes.  Its total number of trees is odd, so there is no tie.
   344	
   345	Clause trees:
   346	{chr(10).join(tree_lines)}
   347	
   348	The baseline input is {inst['baseline']}.  The L0 distance between two bit
   349	strings is the number of positions at which they differ.  Find any candidate
   350	z whose L0 distance from the baseline is at most {inst['l0_budget']} and for
   351	which the ensemble's strict-majority label is True.  Any such input is valid;
   352	you do not have to recover a distinguished planted input.
   353	
   354	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one contiguous
   355	{n}-character bit string in x_0,...,x_{n - 1} order.
   356	Example format (showing syntax, not a claimed solution): <answer>{'0' * n}</answer>
   357	Output nothing else inside the tags."""
   358	    mode = os.environ.get("GV_HINT_MODE")
   359	    if mode == "structural":
   360	        statement += "\n\n" + STRUCTURAL_HINT
   361	    elif mode == "placebo":
   362	        statement += "\n\n" + PLACEBO_HINT
   363	    return statement
   364	
   365	
   366	def parse_answer(text: object) -> object | None:
   367	    if not isinstance(text, str):
   368	        return None
   369	    match = _ANSWER_RE.search(text)
   370	    if not match:
   371	        return None
   372	    body = match.group(1).strip()
   373	    body = re.sub(r"^```(?:text|json)?\s*", "", body, flags=re.I)
   374	    body = re.sub(r"\s*```$", "", body)
   375	    if len(body) >= 2 and body[0] == body[-1] and body[0] in "\"'":
   376	        body = body[1:-1].strip()
   377	    if re.fullmatch(r"[01]+", body):
   378	        return body
   379	    # Tolerate a model spacing or comma-separating individual bits.
   380	    if re.fullmatch(r"[01](?:[\s,]+[01])+", body):
   381	        return "".join(re.findall(r"[01]", body))
   382	    return None
   383	
   384	
   385	def _clause_value(clause: list[int], answer: str) -> bool:
   386	    for literal in clause:
   387	        bit = answer[abs(literal) - 1] == "1"
   388	        if bit == (literal > 0):
   389	            return True
   390	    return False
   391	
   392	
   393	def verify(inst: dict, answer: object) -> tuple[bool, str]:
   394	    if not isinstance(answer, str):
   395	        return False, "answer must be a bit string"
   396	    if answer == "":
   397	        return False, "answer is empty"
   398	    n = inst.get("n")
   399	    if len(answer) < n:
   400	        return False, f"answer is too short: expected {n} bits"
   401	    if len(answer) > n:
   402	        return False, f"answer is too long: expected {n} bits"
   403	    if any(bit not in "01" for bit in answer):
   404	        return False, "answer contains a non-binary character"
   405	
   406	    baseline = inst.get("baseline")
   407	    budget = inst.get("l0_budget")
   408	    if not isinstance(baseline, str) or len(baseline) != n:
   409	        return False, "instance baseline is malformed"
   410	    distance = sum(a != b for a, b in zip(answer, baseline))
   411	    if distance > budget:
   412	        return False, f"L0 distance {distance} exceeds budget {budget}"
   413	
   414	    clauses = inst.get("clause_trees")
   415	    false_trees = inst.get("constant_false_trees")
   416	    if not isinstance(clauses, list) or not isinstance(false_trees, int):
   417	        return False, "instance forest is malformed"
   418	    for tree_index, clause in enumerate(clauses):
   419	        if (
   420	            not isinstance(clause, list)
   421	            or len(clause) != 3
   422	            or any(isinstance(x, bool) or not isinstance(x, int) or x == 0 or abs(x) > n for x in clause)
   423	            or len({abs(x) for x in clause}) != 3
   424	        ):
   425	            return False, "instance contains a malformed clause tree"
   426	        if not _clause_value(clause, answer):
   427	            # Since there are C-1 constant-False trees, one False clause is
   428	            # already enough to prevent a strict True majority.
   429	            return False, f"forest majority is False: clause tree {tree_index} voted False"
   430	    true_votes = len(clauses)
   431	    if true_votes <= (len(clauses) + false_trees) // 2:
   432	        return False, "forest majority is False"
   433	    return True, "ok"
   434	
   435	
   436	def random_candidate(inst: dict, rng: random.Random) -> object:
   437	    n = inst["n"]
   438	    return format(rng.getrandbits(n), f"0{n}b")
   439	
   440	
   441	def search_space(inst: dict) -> int | None:
   442	    return 1 << inst["n"]
   443	
   444	
   445	def enumerate_all(inst: dict) -> int | None:
   446	    space = search_space(inst)
   447	    if space is None or space > _ENUMERATION_CAP:
   448	        return None
   449	    n = inst["n"]
   450	    count = 0
   451	    for value in range(space):
   452	        candidate = format(value, f"0{n}b")
   453	        count += int(verify(inst, candidate)[0])
   454	    return count
   455	
   456	
   457	def canonical_key(inst: dict) -> str:
   458	    """Strong cheap invariant under feature, tree, test, and tag-basis relabelling.
   459	
   460	    Exact isomorphism of the signed 3-uniform incidence structure is not known
   461	    to be cheap.  Colour refinement gives a deterministic structural fingerprint
   462	    and, unlike hashing the rendered tags, also quotients a global GL(d,2)
   463	    change of coordinates on those tags.
   464	    """
   465	    n = inst["n"]
   466	    tags = inst["tags"]
   467	    if len(tags) != n or len(set(tags)) != n:
   468	        raise ValueError("malformed tag list")
   469	    equations = _extract_xor_equations(inst)
   470	    baseline = inst.get("baseline")
   471	    if not isinstance(baseline, str) or len(baseline) != n:
   472	        raise ValueError("malformed baseline")
   473	
   474	    incident: list[list[int]] = [[] for _ in range(n)]
   475	    for edge_index, (indices, _) in enumerate(equations):
   476	        for index in indices:
   477	            incident[index].append(edge_index)
   478	
   479	    # One-dimensional Weisfeiler--Lehman refinement on the bipartite incidence
   480	    # graph, with equation right sides and baseline bits as initial colours.
   481	    variable_colours = [int(bit) for bit in baseline]
   482	    equation_colours = [rhs for _, rhs in equations]
   483	    for _ in range(n + 1):
   484	        variable_signatures = [
   485	            (variable_colours[index], tuple(sorted(equation_colours[e] for e in incident[index])))
   486	            for index in range(n)
   487	        ]
   488	        variable_palette = {
   489	            signature: colour
   490	            for colour, signature in enumerate(sorted(set(variable_signatures)))
   491	        }
   492	        new_variable_colours = [variable_palette[s] for s in variable_signatures]
   493	
   494	        equation_signatures = [
   495	            (rhs, tuple(sorted(new_variable_colours[index] for index in indices)))
   496	            for indices, rhs in equations
   497	        ]
   498	        equation_palette = {
   499	            signature: colour
   500	            for colour, signature in enumerate(sorted(set(equation_signatures)))
   501	        }
   502	        new_equation_colours = [equation_palette[s] for s in equation_signatures]
   503	        if (
   504	            new_variable_colours == variable_colours
   505	            and new_equation_colours == equation_colours
   506	        ):
   507	            break
   508	        variable_colours = new_variable_colours
   509	        equation_colours = new_equation_colours
   510	
   511	    canonical_equations = tuple(
   512	        sorted(
   513	            (rhs, tuple(sorted(variable_colours[index] for index in indices)))
   514	            for indices, rhs in equations
   515	        )
   516	    )
   517	    payload = (
   518	        n,
   519	        inst["tag_bits"],
   520	        tuple(sorted(variable_colours)),
   521	        canonical_equations,
   522	        inst["constant_false_trees"],
   523	        inst["l0_budget"],
   524	        inst["target_label"],
   525	    )
   526	    raw = repr(payload).encode("ascii")
   527	    return hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest()
   528	
   529	
   530	def escalate(params: dict) -> dict | str | None:
   531	    result = dict(params)
   532	    n = int(result.get("n", 31))
   533	    factor = int(result.get("equation_factor", 3))
   534	    if factor < 8:
   535	        result["equation_factor"] = factor + 1
   536	        return result
   537	    if n < 255:
   538	        result["n"] = 2 * n + 1
   539	        result["equation_factor"] = 5
   540	        return result
   541	    return "cap_bound"
   542	
   543	
   544	def _extract_xor_equations(inst: dict) -> list[tuple[tuple[int, int, int], int]]:
   545	    """Recognize the four-tree 3-CNF encoding of every XOR row."""
   546	    groups: dict[tuple[int, int, int], list[list[int]]] = {}
   547	    for clause in inst["clause_trees"]:
   548	        key = tuple(sorted(abs(literal) - 1 for literal in clause))
   549	        groups.setdefault(key, []).append(clause)
   550	    equations = []
   551	    for key, clauses in groups.items():
   552	        if len(clauses) != 4:
   553	            raise ValueError("clause trees do not form four-tree XOR blocks")
   554	        forbidden_patterns = set()
   555	        forbidden_parities = set()
   556	        for clause in clauses:
   557	            by_index = {abs(literal) - 1: literal for literal in clause}
   558	            forbidden = tuple(0 if by_index[index] > 0 else 1 for index in key)
   559	            forbidden_patterns.add(forbidden)
   560	            forbidden_parities.add(forbidden[0] ^ forbidden[1] ^ forbidden[2])
   561	        if len(forbidden_patterns) != 4 or len(forbidden_parities) != 1:
   562	            raise ValueError("malformed XOR clause block")
   563	        forbidden_parity = next(iter(forbidden_parities))
   564	        equations.append((key, 1 ^ forbidden_parity))
   565	    return equations
   566	
   567	
   568	def _reference_gaussian(inst: dict) -> tuple[str | None, dict]:
   569	    """Polynomial Track-B reference solver, with elementary bit-op accounting."""
   570	    started = time.perf_counter()
   571	    n = inst["n"]
   572	    equations = _extract_xor_equations(inst)
   573	    variable_mask = (1 << n) - 1
   574	    pivots: dict[int, int] = {}
   575	    bit_operations = 0
   576	    row_xors = 0
   577	    pivot_tests = 0
   578	    for indices, rhs in equations:
   579	        row = (1 << indices[0]) | (1 << indices[1]) | (1 << indices[2]) | (rhs << n)
   580	        while row & variable_mask:
   581	            pivot = (row & variable_mask).bit_length() - 1
   582	            pivot_tests += 1
   583	            bit_operations += 1
   584	            if pivot in pivots:
   585	                row ^= pivots[pivot]
   586	                row_xors += 1
   587	                bit_operations += n + 1
   588	            else:
   589	                pivots[pivot] = row
   590	                break
   591	        if not (row & variable_mask) and ((row >> n) & 1):
   592	            return None, {
   593	                "wall_clock_sec": time.perf_counter() - started,
   594	                "bit_operations": bit_operations,
   595	                "row_xors": row_xors,
   596	                "pivot_tests": pivot_tests,
   597	                "rank": len(pivots),
   598	            }
   599	
   600	    solution = 0
   601	    for pivot, row in sorted(pivots.items()):
   602	        lower = row & ((1 << pivot) - 1)
   603	        rhs = (row >> n) & 1
   604	        bit = rhs ^ ((lower & solution).bit_count() & 1)
   605	        bit_operations += pivot + 2
   606	        if bit:
   607	            solution |= 1 << pivot
   608	    answer = "".join("1" if (solution >> i) & 1 else "0" for i in range(n))
   609	    return answer, {
   610	        "wall_clock_sec": time.perf_counter() - started,
   611	        "bit_operations": bit_operations,
   612	        "row_xors": row_xors,
   613	        "pivot_tests": pivot_tests,
   614	        "rank": len(pivots),
   615	    }
   616	
   617	
   618	def _equation_violations(equations: list[tuple[tuple[int, int, int], int]], bits: list[int]) -> int:
   619	    return sum((bits[a] ^ bits[b] ^ bits[c]) != rhs for (a, b, c), rhs in equations)
   620	
   621	
   622	def _candidate_satisfies_equations(
   623	    candidate: str, equations: list[tuple[tuple[int, int, int], int]]
   624	) -> bool:
   625	    """Fast exact equivalent of the forest vote for prevalidated instances."""
   626	    for (a, b, c), rhs in equations:
   627	        if ((candidate[a] == "1") ^ (candidate[b] == "1") ^ (candidate[c] == "1")) != bool(rhs):
   628	            return False
   629	    return True
   630	
   631	
   632	def _attack_outlier_tag_degree(inst: dict) -> tuple[str, int]:
   633	    equations = _extract_xor_equations(inst)
   634	    degree = [0] * inst["n"]
   635	    for indices, _ in equations:
   636	        for index in indices:
   637	            degree[index] += 1
   638	    ordered = sorted(degree)
   639	    median = ordered[len(ordered) // 2]
   640	    return "".join("1" if value > median else "0" for value in degree), len(equations) * 3
   641	
   642	
   643	def _attack_greedy_single_sweep(inst: dict) -> tuple[str, int]:
   644	    equations = _extract_xor_equations(inst)
   645	    bits = [0] * inst["n"]
   646	    operations = 0
   647	    current = _equation_violations(equations, bits)
   648	    operations += len(equations)
   649	    # A deliberately plausible no-backtracking local repair: keep a flip only
   650	    # if it strictly improves the number of satisfied clause blocks.
   651	    for index in range(inst["n"]):
   652	        bits[index] ^= 1
   653	        trial = _equation_violations(equations, bits)
   654	        operations += len(equations)
   655	        if trial < current:
   656	            current = trial
   657	        else:
   658	            bits[index] ^= 1
   659	    return "".join(map(str, bits)), operations
