    63	  T_9: +5 -3 -1
    64	  T_10: +7 -3 -2
    65	  T_11: +6 +5 -2
    66	  T_12: +2 -4 -1
    67	  T_13: -4 -3 -6
    68	  T_14: -5 -6 -2
    69	  T_15: -7 -3 +2
    70	  T_16: +5 +2 -6
    71	  T_17: +3 -1 -5
    72	  T_18: +1 +3 +5
    73	  T_19: +7 +2 +3
    74	  T_20: -6 -7 -1
    75	  T_21: +1 +4 +2
    76	  T_22: -5 +7 -4
    77	  T_23: -7 +6 +1
    78	  T_24: -2 -1 +4
    79	  T_25: +5 -4 -7
    80	  T_26: +6 -3 +4
    81	  T_27: +5 +7 +4
    82	
    83	The baseline input is 0000000.  The L0 distance between two bit
    84	strings is the number of positions at which they differ.  Find any candidate
    85	z whose L0 distance from the baseline is at most 7 and for
    86	which the ensemble's strict-majority label is True.  Any such input is valid;
    87	you do not have to recover a distinguished planted input.
    88	
    89	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one contiguous
    90	7-character bit string in x_0,...,x_6 order.
    91	Example format (showing syntax, not a claimed solution): <answer>0000000</answer>
    92	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    93	```
    94	
    95	The retained answer is `1101010`. `verify(inst, "1101010")` returns `(True, "ok")`; deleting its final bit returns `(False, "answer is too short: expected 7 bits")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by grouping the four clauses on each feature triple into one parity equation.
