 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Affine-XOR extraction from tree ensembles
     2	
     3	Trust status: the generator and all deterministic gates pass, but the required four-vendor oracle experiment is **not measured**. OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 “key limit exceeded” on every bare, structural-hint, and placebo call. The script-owned error transcripts are retained; they are not counted as model failures and do not support a hardness verdict.
     4	
     5	| Profile field | Value |
     6	|---|---|
     7	| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
     8	| Native domain | logic |
     9	| Object regime | finite discrete |
    10	| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
    11	| Certificate | integer tuple (a fixed-order Boolean input, serialized as a bit string) |
    12	| Intuition | change of variables: repeated four-tree blocks are affine XOR equations on public binary-vector tags |
    13	| Domain essentiality | native |
    14	| Reduction | paper-licensed: Section 6, Theorem 1 |
    15	
    16	## What the family asks
    17	
    18	The source is Huang, Zhao, and Huang, [“Embedding and Extraction of Knowledge in Tree Ensemble Classifiers”](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.08281). Section 2 defines decision-tree paths and ensemble voting. Section 6, Eq. (10), asks for an input (x') within an (L_0) budget of a training input that traverses a suspected target joint path. Theorem 1 proves NP-completeness by making one decision tree for every 3-SAT clause and adding one fewer constant-False tree; the ensemble votes True exactly when every clause tree accepts.
    19	
    20	An instance here contains those same objects: Boolean features, three-test clause trees, constant-False trees, an all-zero baseline, and the full-feature (L_0) budget used by the theorem’s reduction. The solver returns any fixed-order input bit string that makes the ensemble vote True. `verify` checks its shape, exact Hamming distance, every literal path, and the exact integer majority. It never reads `inst["answer"]`.
    21	
    22	Generation is inverse. The generator first samples a quadratic Boolean form $q$ and a linear form $\ell$ on the nonzero vectors of $\mathbb F_2^d$. It retains the input $q(v)\oplus\ell(v)$, creates identities on triples $(u,v,u\oplus v)$, compiles each identity into four 3-CNF clause trees, and shuffles feature and tree order. The certificate is known before the forest exists. `render` groups trees with the same three tested features and canonicalizes only their display order; every parenthesized tree remains a separate vote.
    23	
    24	## Why Track B
    25	
    26	This is not a Track-A claim. The paper’s exact general extraction method uses SMT, and Theorem 1 establishes NP-completeness; Section 6.1 also warns that the joint-path disjunction may be exponential. On this promised affine distribution, however, four-tree XOR recognition followed by GF(2) Gaussian elimination is polynomial, $O(en^2)$ in the counted bit-operation model. It solved 8/8 shipping instances at an average **1,140,137 scalar bit operations** and **0.0040 s** per instance in the retained final run. A separate domain-standard DPLL solver with unit propagation also solved 8/8: **65,873 clause scans**, eight search nodes, and **0.0198 s** per instance on average.
    27	
    28	The compact route is different: assign the seven basis-tagged features zero and use the compulsory tagged identities in increasing Hamming weight. Each of the other 120 features costs two binary XORs, for exactly **240 XORs**. The challenge is recognizing that change of variables in a 635-block, 2,540-tree forest without a sandbox. The paper’s PTIME embedding Algorithms 1–2 are therefore not used as a false Track-A hardness claim. Its loss/activation outlier methods in Section 6.2 are only prefilters and are explicitly allowed to produce false alarms.
    29	
    30	## Worked demo
    31	
    32	This is `make_instance(n=7, equation_factor=1, seed=7)` rendered in full:
    33	
    34	```text
    35	Knowledge extraction from a Boolean tree ensemble
    36	
    37	There are 7 Boolean input features x_0,...,x_6.  A candidate input is
    38	a bit string z of length 7; its character at zero-based position i is x_i.
    39	The features also have the following public, fixed 3-bit vector tags.  Angle
    40	brackets delimit a tag and are not part of the feature value:
    41	  x_0=<010>  x_1=<111>  x_2=<011>  x_3=<101>  x_4=<001>  x_5=<110>  x_6=<100>
    42	
    43	The ensemble contains the 28 clause trees listed below and
    44	27 additional constant-False trees.  In a clause
    45	tree, +j means the literal x_(j-1)=1 and -j means x_(j-1)=0; signed feature
    46	numbers are therefore one-based even though bit-string positions are
    47	zero-based.  A listed tree tests its three literals from left to right, returns
    48	True immediately when a literal is true, and returns False if all three are
    49	false.  Reordering the three tests would not change that tree's Boolean
    50	function.  The complete ensemble returns the label with a strict majority of
    51	votes.  Its total number of trees is odd, so there is no tie.
    52	
    53	For compact display, each B-line groups the four separate clause trees that
    54	test the same three features.  Parentheses delimit individual trees and the
    55	vertical bar separates them; every parenthesized tree contributes one vote.
    56	The angle-bracket header repeats those features' public tags in increasing
    57	binary order.  Blocks are ordered lexicographically by their tag triples.
    58	Grouping and ordering are presentation only and do not add a vote or constraint.
    59	
    60	Clause-tree blocks:
    61	  B_0 <001,010,011>: (+5 +1 +3) | (+5 -1 -3) | (-5 +1 -3) | (-5 -1 +3)
    62	  B_1 <001,100,101>: (+5 +7 +4) | (+5 -7 -4) | (-5 +7 -4) | (-5 -7 +4)
    63	  B_2 <001,110,111>: (+5 +6 -2) | (+5 -6 +2) | (-5 +6 +2) | (-5 -6 -2)
    64	  B_3 <010,100,110>: (+1 +7 -6) | (+1 -7 +6) | (-1 +7 +6) | (-1 -7 -6)
    65	  B_4 <010,101,111>: (+1 +4 +2) | (+1 -4 -2) | (-1 +4 -2) | (-1 -4 +2)
    66	  B_5 <011,100,111>: (+3 +7 +2) | (+3 -7 -2) | (-3 +7 -2) | (-3 -7 +2)
    67	  B_6 <011,101,110>: (+3 +4 -6) | (+3 -4 +6) | (-3 +4 +6) | (-3 -4 -6)
    68	
    69	The baseline input is 0000000.  The L0 distance between two bit
    70	strings is the number of positions at which they differ.  Find any candidate
    71	z whose L0 distance from the baseline is at most 7 and for
    72	which the ensemble's strict-majority label is True.  Any such input is valid;
    73	you do not have to recover a distinguished planted input.
    74	
    75	Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one contiguous
    76	7-character bit string in x_0,...,x_6 order.
    77	Example format (showing syntax, not a claimed solution): <answer>0000000</answer>
    78	Output nothing else inside the tags.
    79	```
    80	
    81	The retained answer is `1101010`. `verify(inst, "1101010")` returns `(True, "ok")`; deleting its final bit returns `(False, "answer is too short: expected 7 bits")`. A person can solve this demo on paper by grouping the four clauses on each feature triple into one parity equation.
    82	
    83	## Difficulty
    84	
    85	| Preset | Features | Equation factor | Clause trees | Constant-False trees | Answer bits | Status |
    86	|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
    87	| demo | 7 | 1 | 28 | 27 | 7 | hand-scale; skipped by hardener |
    88	| easy | 31 | 3 | 372 | 371 | 31 | deterministic gates pass; oracle unavailable |
    89	| medium | 63 | 4 | 1,008 | 1,007 | deterministic gates pass; oracle unavailable |
    90	| hard | 127 | 5 | 2,540 | 2,539 | **provisional shipping preset** |
    91	
    92	Escalation first raises redundant-equation crowding at fixed answer length. Once factor 8 is exhausted, it doubles the ambient feature set only when both the 256-atom and 300-operation caps still hold; from the shipping size, the next doubling correctly returns `cap_bound` because its compact route would require 494 XORs. G7 nevertheless builds that 255-feature instance to test scalability.
    93	
    94	## Gate measurements
    95	
    96	| Gate | Result | Measurement |
